await writeFile(L26+'notes.md',`l26 changes only dust optical response from the owner-accepted l25. All stellar light, grades, dust positions, native HERITAGE mean columns and OGLE foreground geometry remain unchanged. A single global unresolved-clump scale is ${num(selected.eta)} within 0–30. Dust strength stays 1.2.\n\n| Fixed l25 lane pixels / adjacent bar | Dimming | Relative R/G | Relative B/G |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n${row('Horálek',photo)}\n${row('l25',baseline.l25)}\n${row('l26',candidate)}\n\nDeclared tolerances are 3 percentage points in dimming and 2% in each lane color ratio. The l26 errors are ${(100*(num(candidate.depth)-num(photo.depth))).toFixed(2)} percentage points, ${(100*(num(candidate.rg)/num(photo.rg)-1)).toFixed(2)}% and ${(100*(num(candidate.bg)/num(photo.bg)-1)).toFixed(2)}%. They do not all pass. Whole-bar color errors are ${barErrors.map(v=>(100*v).toFixed(2)+'%').join(' and ')}, both within 2%.\n\nThe dust is visibly lighter, but the brown lanes wash out. The physical dust coordinates are preserved; the visible lane-location criterion fails. The foreground-only alternative failed the native-resolution check and is not used. The unresolved-clump treatment keeps the Gordon law inside clumps and gives a greyer ensemble attenuation; its fitted scale is an assumption at the upper bound, not a measurement. Side views remain coherent.\n\nTraining and held-out RMS are ${num(res.trainRelativeRms).toFixed(6)} and ${num(res.heldoutRelativeRms).toFixed(6)}; they are reported, not used as gates. The tiny pilot checks improvement, since aliasing changes apparent lane depth. Final acceptance uses native 512 px bar rays. This is a completed diagnostic for review; l25 remains the accepted base. No slab bake or full-resolution fit ran.\n`);
await writeFile(L26+'method.md',`The lane mask is frozen from accepted l25, not reselected for each candidate. In the catalogue-masked bar, it contains native HERITAGE A_V at least 0.2 mag and l25 local green transmission at most 0.85. Nearby bar light is a 3 arcminute Gaussian interpolation from low-column pixels with A_V at most 0.15 mag. Both images use the same locations, half-arcminute signal smoothing and registration. Dimming is measured in decoded linear display green; lane R/G and B/G are ratios relative to the adjacent displayed bar. This measures appearance in Horálek's processed image, not an independent optical-depth map. The 1944 native lane pixels and masks are saved locally.\n\nThe first model lowered effective foreground fractions only within the lower 34-percentile width of each complete [Skowron et al. 2021](https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-4365/abcb81) red-clump aperture. Those widths are differential reddening and stellar scatter, not errors on the median. It failed at native resolution and is not applied. The final model retains every l25 cloud centre, width, weight, regional fraction and 250 pc depth distribution exactly, including the Chen E(B-V)=0.06 foreground.\n\nThe final optical response follows the absorption-only Poisson unresolved-cloud model of [Natta and Panagia 1984](https://doi.org/10.1086/162681): T_lambda = exp[-N (1-exp(-tau_clump,lambda))], with N tau_clump,V equal to the mean V optical depth. The single global clump-depth scale eta multiplies each native mean column, activated smoothly between A_V=0.2 and 0.5 mag. Thus kappa_effective,lambda = kappa_Gordon,lambda (1-exp(-eta tau_V gate kappa_Gordon,lambda))/(eta tau_V gate kappa_Gordon,lambda). The mean column is summed over all five depth quadrature nodes before applying this response, so opacity does not depend on their numerical weights. All cameras use the same fixed 3D coefficients; there is no Earth-only mask or per-pixel photo transfer.\n\nThe inherited [Gordon et al. 2003](https://doi.org/10.1086/376774) broad optical proxies are R/G/B extinction coefficients 0.75, 1 and 1+1/3.41. They describe extinction inside a clump. The ensemble response is less opaque and greyer. No brown RGB tint, stellar recoloring, random cloud positions or source-column renormalization is added. HERITAGE's native columns retain the single Bell scale 0.88580915749 and global strength 1.2. Scattering back into the beam is omitted. The porosity scale and its density activation are adopted priors, not measured structure; the fitted upper bound and failed color criterion limit its interpretation.\n`);
const location=rec(rec(q).laneLocation),oldLocation=rec(location.l25VersusPhoto),newLocation=rec(location.l26VersusPhoto);await appendFile(L26+'notes.md',`\nIndependently detected brown-lane overlap with the photo is ${num(oldLocation.intersectionOverUnion).toFixed(3)} in l25 and ${num(newLocation.intersectionOverUnion).toFixed(3)} in l26. The physical dust coordinates are exactly preserved; this separate statistic checks their visible contrast rather than inferring lane agreement from the source geometry.\n`);
